          { name: "Renter Economic Security and Housing Stability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 61.1% renters and $34,849 median household income, the majority of HD-2 constituents are renting on a budget that leaves almost no margin for rent increases, habitability failures, or unexpected costs. This is not a market where households can absorb landlord-side price pressure or exercise mobility — it is a district where housing instability is a proximate risk for a majority of the electorate. Concrete policy positions on renter protections, habitability enforcement, eviction process reform, and affordable housing development are not ideological signals in this district — they are direct responses to the economic conditions of the people who voted in 2023. A candidate who owns this issue with specificity, rather than treating it as one bullet point among many, is addressing the single most widespread economic vulnerability in the constituency." },
          { name: "Black Community Institutional Investment and Service Delivery", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 64.24% Black and 14.9% college attainment — one of the lowest credential rates in any competitive district — HD-2's majority constituency depends heavily on public institutions for healthcare access, workforce development, and economic mobility pathways that credentialed, higher-income households access privately. The 52% win in 2023 suggests that a meaningful share of Black voters either did not turn out or chose the Republican candidate, which in a district this demographically concentrated is a signal worth investigating directly. A legislative agenda that delivers visible, specific results on community healthcare access, public school investment, and workforce training — and communicates those results back to the community with transparency — is the foundation of a durable hold rather than a perpetually fragile 52%. Authentic presence in Black community institutions, not just campaign-season visibility, is the credibility prerequisite for holding this seat." },
          { name: "Hispanic Constituency Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "At 9.29% Hispanic, this community is small enough to overlook in a district whose dominant demographic story is the Black majority — but in a 52-48 race, a community representing roughly one in eleven residents is mathematically decisive if its turnout or partisan alignment shifts even modestly. The $34,849 district median income and 61.1% renter rate suggest that Hispanic residents in HD-2 are likely concentrated in the same economic precarity as the broader constituency, meaning their concerns around housing stability, workforce access, and healthcare access overlap substantially with the district's majority issues rather than requiring a separate policy framework. The risk is not in engaging this community — it is in engaging them performatively rather than substantively, which generates cynicism rather than coalition. A candidate who treats Hispanic constituent service as an extension of the same economic security agenda driving the rest of the district builds coalition; one who treats it as a separate ethnic outreach track misses the structural overlap." },
          { name: "Abstract Economic Messaging Disconnected from Renter and Low-Income Reality", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with $34,849 median income and 61.1% renters, campaign messaging pitched at homeowner asset-building, small business ownership, or middle-class credential pathways reaches a minority of the actual electorate at best and reads as tone-deaf at worst. The 14.9% college attainment rate means that credential-based economic messaging — 'invest in education so you can get a better job' — does not resonate as an immediate material answer for a constituency managing today's rent on today's income. Candidates who import generic economic uplift messaging without anchoring it to the specific conditions of a low-income renter majority are not campaigning in HD-2 — they are campaigning in their imagination of it. In a 52-48 district, that abstraction is not a missed opportunity; it is an active vulnerability." },
          { name: "Treating 52% as a Stable Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 52% result in 2023 is a single-cycle data point in a district whose structural conditions — concentrated poverty, high renter vulnerability, low educational attainment — create persistent volatility in turnout and candidate preference. The 48% opposition in a district that is 64.24% Black cannot be explained by demographic partisanship alone; it reflects either meaningful Republican crossover performance, significant low-turnout gaps among the Democratic base, or both — and neither condition is self-correcting without active intervention. A candidate who campaigns the 2025 cycle as though the 52% represents a consolidated coalition rather than a narrow and contingent win will find that the structural vulnerabilities have compounded rather than eased. The strategic mandate is to expand the margin, not protect it — because at 52%, there is no margin left to protect." }
