        memoHeadline: "A 52-48 Knife-Fight in a Majority-Black, Low-Income, Renter-Heavy Louisiana District Where Economic Precarity Is the Central Electoral Fact",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "LA-HD-2 is a district whose demography tells a precise story about concentrated economic vulnerability: 64.24% Black, $34,849 median household income, 61.1% renters, and only 14.9% of adults with a college degree. These four numbers in combination describe a constituency that is predominantly working-class, predominantly renting, and predominantly without the credential-based earnings premium that produces economic stability in the modern Louisiana economy. The 52-48 result Jackson achieved in 2023 is not a comfortable win — it is a district on a knife's edge, where a swing of roughly two percentage points in either direction produces a different outcome. In a district this close, with an income floor this low and a renter rate this high, the candidate who wins is the one who most credibly addresses the material conditions of daily life for people who do not own their home, do not hold a degree, and are managing a household on under $35,000 a year.",
          "The 21.9% white population and 9.29% Hispanic population represent meaningful secondary constituencies whose participation and alignment will determine whether Jackson's 52% becomes a durable hold or a one-cycle anomaly. The four reported categories sum to approximately 97.08%, leaving a small residual gap that may include American Indian or Alaska Native residents and should not be ignored in outreach planning. The 40.5 median age signals a working-age district, not a retiree community — which means the electorate's concerns center on wages, rental costs, healthcare access, and whether public institutions are functioning for people who cannot afford private alternatives. A 61.1% renter rate is not merely a demographic footnote; it is the single most important economic vulnerability indicator in this district, because renters at $34,849 median income are one landlord decision or one health crisis away from housing instability. Any candidate who does not engage that reality with concrete policy specificity is campaigning for a different district than the one that actually exists."
        ],
