          { name: "Jobs, Wages, and Economic Mobility for Young Black Residents", tag: "lean-into", why: "With a median age of 33.8, 75.8% Black population, and household income of $34,734 — well below both Louisiana and national medians — HD-3 is a young, working-age Black district where economic mobility is the defining generational concern. Workforce development, living wage standards, access to job training, and pathways out of poverty are not abstract policy questions here; they are the material stakes of every legislative session for constituents who are young enough to benefit from structural change but economically constrained enough that inaction compounds over time. A representative who builds a specific record on economic advancement for working-class Black residents in this income and age bracket is building the core of a durable primary coalition." },
          { name: "Community Presence and Constituent Services Depth", tag: "careful", why: "In an uncontested general election district, the representative's vulnerability is entirely internal — a challenger who outperforms on constituent visibility, casework responsiveness, and community presence can build a primary threat even without policy differentiation. The 11.7% college attainment rate means the district evaluates representation experientially, not documentarily: residents know whether they can reach their representative, whether problems get solved, and whether the legislator shows up in the community consistently. This is not a criticism of any particular incumbent's record — it is a structural feature of low-income, high-renter constituencies where institutional trust is earned through proximity and reliability. A candidate who maintains genuine community saturation is insulated; one who governs at distance is exposed." },
          { name: "Abstract Policy Positioning Without Material Anchors", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with 11.7% college attainment and $34,734 median income, legislative communication framed around procedural accomplishments, committee assignments, or ideological positioning will not land with the constituency. Voters in HD-3 need to understand what changed in their neighborhood, their rent, their job options, or their children's school — not what bills were filed or what caucus coalitions were built. A candidate who defaults to insider-legislative framing as evidence of effectiveness is communicating in a language that fails to translate for the majority of the district, and in a competitive primary that failure is a direct opening for a challenger who speaks in concrete terms." },
          { name: "Treating the Uncontested Result as Evidence of Security", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% unopposed result in 2023 is not a mandate — it is an artifact of primary consolidation and general election non-competition that can dissolve quickly if a credible primary challenger emerges with stronger community ties, a specific material grievance, or better-resourced constituent service operation. In majority-Black, low-income, high-renter districts across the South, incumbents who interpret uncontested wins as political safety and reduce their community engagement accordingly are frequently surprised by primary challengers who built quietly while the incumbent governed from a distance. The 75.8% Black constituency at this income and renter level has both the motivation and the organizational infrastructure — through churches, community groups, and neighborhood networks — to produce a competitive primary whenever a convincing alternative presents itself." }
