          { name: "Black and Hispanic Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The 8.78% Black and 5.61% Hispanic communities together represent roughly 14% of HD-24's population and are almost certainly the primary demographic anchor of the 40% Democratic share — meaning they are not a marginal constituency but the organized opposition's electoral base. A candidate who ignores them cedes the 40% to an opponent; a candidate who engages them with authentic constituent service on economic mobility, healthcare access, and workforce opportunity has the opportunity to peel off persuadable voters and suppress the opposition ceiling. The engagement must be specific and grounded in actual community concerns rather than performative — at 16.6% district-wide college attainment, these communities face real workforce and economic barriers that have policy solutions. The risk of getting this wrong is not just lost votes; it is cementing the 40% as a permanent, energized opposition in a district where the demographic trend line may not favor complacency." },
          { name: "Abstract Culture-War Framing as a Substitute for Economic Policy", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 16.6% of adults hold a college degree and the median household income is $61,423, the constituency's primary lived concerns are economic — job stability, healthcare costs, housing affordability, and whether their standard of living is improving. Campaigns that lead with abstract cultural or ideological positioning rather than specific economic commitments risk being perceived as indifferent to the material pressures that are likely generating the 40% opposition in the first place. A 35.5-year median age means the district's median voter is young enough to be evaluating on economic mobility, not settled enough to vote on identity alone. Candidates who treat culture-war framing as a turnout substitute for economic substance in this district are misreading their own constituency." },
          { name: "Treating 60% as a Safe and Stable Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "Schamerhorn's 60% in 2023 is a majority, not a mandate — and the district's demographic profile contains several destabilizing factors that make complacency dangerous. The 27.4% renter rate means a quarter of households have no homeownership anchor to conventional conservative coalition politics; the 35.5-year median age means the district is young enough to shift preferences as economic conditions change; and the 14% combined Black and Hispanic population provides an organized opposition base that, with higher turnout or modest persuasion, can compress a 60-40 margin significantly. In Louisiana's competitive legislative landscape, a 20-point margin can narrow to single digits in a single cycle if a credible challenger runs on economic security themes against an incumbent who has not actively tended the constituency. This seat requires active engagement, not the quiet confidence of a landslide." }
