          { name: "Workforce Development & Local Job Quality", tag: "lean-into", why: "The district's low college-education rate of 12.7% signals a constituency heavily dependent on non-degree pathways to economic mobility. This is not a skills mismatch problem to be solved by exhorting education; it reflects a real population for whom vocational training, apprenticeships, credential programs, and direct job placement matter enormously. A candidate who has actively supported or delivered apprenticeships, manufacturing employment, or skilled trades pathways will have tangible proof of alignment with this district's actual labor-market needs." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access & Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "At $54,206 median income, healthcare cost-sharing and insurance gaps are genuine vulnerabilities, but healthcare is a deeply polarizing issue in American politics. A candidate should acknowledge the access problem directly—rural or suburban healthcare deserts, insurance gaps—without leading with ideological solutions that alienate the 73% white population or the broad political mainstream of an unopposed-incumbent district. Lean on local delivery and cost control rather than systemic reimagining." },
          { name: "Immigration & Border Policy", tag: "avoid", why: "Hispanic / Latino residents comprise only 5.86% of the district, and hard-line immigration rhetoric risks needless alienation of a minority population without offsetting electoral gain in a district where the incumbent won unopposed. The small Hispanic proportion means this is not a salience driver, and messaging that highlights divisiveness on immigration offers no strategic advantage." },
          { name: "Higher Education Expansion & Tuition Policy", tag: "avoid", why: "With only 12.7% college-educated adults, the district skews heavily toward residents who completed their education years ago or chose non-degree pathways. Messaging centered on college affordability or tuition forgiveness will feel disconnected from the lived reality of a constituency for whom college is neither imminent nor necessarily the pathway to stability. Resources and rhetoric are better spent on workforce training and existing-job quality." }
