          { name: "Constituent Service Across the Full Demographic Spectrum", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 16.08% Black population almost certainly accounts for most or all of the 15% Democratic vote share, meaning this community is not part of the winning coalition but is fully present in the district and entitled to responsive representation. The 5.02% Hispanic and 2.97% Asian communities are small in absolute terms but represent real households with real concerns around economic opportunity, healthcare, and education. A representative holding 85% of the vote has no electoral incentive to ignore these communities — and a strong constituent-service record across demographic lines is the kind of governing capital that creates long-term incumbency strength and insulates against redistricting vulnerability." },
          { name: "Renter Community Economic Pressures", tag: "careful", why: "The 21.8% renter rate in HD-48 represents a constituency that is more economically precarious than the homeowning majority and likely skews younger and more racially diverse — meaning their housing stability and affordability concerns are real but structurally different from those of homeowners. Any policy positioning on housing must be calibrated to avoid framing renter interests as in conflict with homeowner interests, since the homeowning 78% is the core electoral coalition. The right approach is policy that increases housing supply and quality broadly — framed as community investment — rather than renter-specific protections that the homeowning majority might read as regulatory burden." },
          { name: "Treating the 85% Margin as a Mandate for Neglect", tag: "avoid", why: "An 85% electoral result can produce a governing style that treats the non-coalition quarter of the district as irrelevant, but this is both a governance failure and a long-term strategic vulnerability. Louisiana's legislative districts are subject to periodic redistricting, and a representative with no constituent relationships across racial and economic lines is more exposed when district lines shift than one who has built broad-based credibility. The 16% Black constituency, 5% Hispanic community, and renter population are not electoral threats in the current configuration, but they are real constituents whose unmet concerns become organizing material for any future challenger with a different profile." },
          { name: "Abstract Ideological Positioning Over Concrete Local Issues", tag: "avoid", why: "At 24% college attainment and a working-class income median of $72,627, HD-48's voters are not primarily motivated by policy abstraction or ideological signaling — they respond to candidates who demonstrate specific knowledge of their material circumstances. A legislative record dominated by culture-war positioning or national-partisan messaging without accompanying action on property insurance, workforce pipelines, healthcare access, or infrastructure will feel disconnected from what this constituency actually needs. In a supermajority seat like this one, the primary electoral risk is not losing to a Democrat — it is losing credibility with the base by appearing more interested in statewide profile-building than in the district's specific working-class economic concerns." }
