        issues: [
          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $57,045 median household income and only 16.4% college attainment, HD-60's constituency is predominantly working-class adults for whom wages, healthcare affordability, and household cost pressures are the central governing concerns. This is not an abstract policy audience — these are 41-year-old homeowners and renters calculating whether their income covers rising costs, and they will evaluate Martinez on whether her legislative record produces concrete relief. Workforce development, Medicaid access, and support for industries that employ non-college workers are the specific policy domains where a legislator can demonstrate tangible value to this constituency." },
          { name: "Black Community Institutional Investment", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 40.62% Black residents, nearly half the district has a direct stake in whether their representative is fighting for equitable healthcare access, economic opportunity, and criminal justice reform at the state level — the specific domains where Louisiana's historical record creates ongoing material disparities. An uncontested win does not mean this community is fully satisfied; it may mean there was no viable alternative, which is a weaker form of loyalty that erodes if Martinez's legislative record fails to deliver visible results. Consistent, specific engagement with Black community institutions — churches, HBCUs if proximate, civic organizations — is the infrastructure that converts an uncontested margin into durable political strength." },
          { name: "Constituent Visibility in a Safe Seat", tag: "careful", why: "Unopposed incumbents in majority-minority, working-class districts face a structural temptation to under-invest in constituent engagement because no electoral threat is visible — but in HD-60, at $57,045 income and 16.4% college attainment, economic frustration can consolidate quickly into a primary challenge or a third-party effort if residents conclude their representative is absent. The 41.1 median age means this is an electorate old enough to have watched previous representatives go through the motions, and skeptical of performance that is not grounded in tangible local outcomes. Martinez should treat constituent visibility — town halls, district office accessibility, specific legislative wins that are communicated clearly — as a strategic priority, not an optional supplement to Baton Rouge work." },
          { name: "Treating the Uncontested Result as a Mandate", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% result in an unopposed race carries zero information about constituent satisfaction — it reflects only the absence of organized opposition, which is a product of electoral geography, party recruitment failures, and resource calculations by opponents, not a referendum on Martinez's performance. In a district with 40.62% Black residents and a working-class income base, a well-funded primary challenger who activates economic frustration or community trust concerns could emerge rapidly if the incumbent is seen as complacent or disconnected. Governing as though 100% uncontested means 100% approval is the fastest path to creating the very vulnerability that currently does not exist." },
          { name: "Abstract Partisan Messaging Divorced from Material Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "At 16.4% college attainment and $57,045 median income, HD-60's constituency is not primarily engaged by ideological or national partisan framing — they are engaged by whether state government is making their lives materially better. A communications strategy built around national Democratic talking points or abstract policy positioning will land poorly in a district where the practical questions are healthcare cost, local school quality, and whether the roads and utilities work. Martinez's credibility in HD-60 depends on translating any legislative activity into specific, local, tangible terms that a working-class constituent with no college background can immediately connect to their household situation." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Unopposed in a Majority-Black, Low-Income Louisiana District: Martinez Holds a Safe Seat Where Economic Anxiety and Community Trust Are the Only Real Competitors",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "HD-60 is a majority-Black district in Louisiana where the 2023 result — 100% uncontested — tells you almost nothing about electoral competition and almost everything about political geography. With 40.62% Black residents and 51.43% white residents, the district is racially split nearly in half, yet the unopposed Democratic result suggests either a deeply blue-leaning constituency or an electorate that has consolidated around Martinez in a way that discourages Republican investment. The $57,045 median household income sits modestly above Louisiana's statewide median but still reflects a working-class economic base, and the 16.4% college attainment rate — well below national averages — signals a constituency whose primary concerns are material and immediate: jobs, healthcare costs, household affordability, and public services that function reliably. An unopposed incumbent should not read an uncontested race as endorsement of her legislative record; it is an absence of organized opposition, which is a different and more fragile form of political security.",
          "The district's renter rate of 21% is relatively low, meaning the homeowner majority has a direct stake in property values, local infrastructure, and the kind of stable neighborhood governance that depends on responsive state-level representation. The 41.1 median age reflects a middle-aged constituency — not a young, volatile electorate and not an aging fixed-income community, but adults in peak earning and caregiving years who are acutely sensitive to healthcare costs, wage stagnation, and the cost of raising children or supporting elderly parents on a working-class income. The demographic gap between the four reported categories sums to approximately 96.98%, leaving a small residual that may include American Indian or multiracial residents worth acknowledging at the constituent level. The strategic reality of HD-60 is not about defeating a challenger — it is about governing a majority-Black, working-class district in a way that makes a future challenge impossible to mount, which requires delivering visible, specific results on economic security and community infrastructure."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Govern visibly on economic security: at $57,045 household income and 16.4% college attainment, constituents are measuring their representative by whether state policy produces lower healthcare costs, better jobs, and functional public services — not by partisan positioning.",
          "Invest in the Black community's specific institutional needs: with 40.62% Black residents in a state with deep historical inequities, constituent trust is earned through consistent attention to healthcare access, criminal justice outcomes, and economic opportunity — not assumed from party affiliation.",
          "Use the uncontested margin as governing capital, not complacency: the absence of a 2023 opponent is an opportunity to build a legislative record so specific and locally visible that future opposition cannot gain traction, not a signal that the seat is permanently safe.",
          "Engage the homeowner majority (79%+ by implication of the 21% renter rate) on neighborhood infrastructure, property values, and local service delivery — these are the pocketbook concerns that drive off-cycle accountability and define whether an incumbent is seen as effective."
        ]
