        issues: [
          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 25.3% college attainment — well below the national average — and a $76,920 median household income, HD-71 is a district of skilled trades workers, service professionals, and working families whose economic concerns are immediate and concrete. Healthcare costs, job quality, and wage growth are the material issues for a constituency at this income and education intersection. The 29% renter rate adds a housing affordability dimension: nearly a third of residents are not yet homeowners, and for younger households at the 35.0 median age, the path to ownership is a live economic question. A legislator who speaks specifically to workforce investment, healthcare cost containment, and housing market conditions is addressing the real economic stakes of the people who actually live here." },
          { name: "Black and Hispanic Constituency Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 10.7% Black population and 8.62% Hispanic population together represent roughly 19% of HD-71 — and in a district where the Republican margin is 14 points, not 40, these communities are a genuine electoral variable rather than a structural footnote. In a 57-43 result, any candidate who consolidates minority community turnout and improves on 2023 Democratic performance by even modest margins shifts the competitive landscape materially. Beyond electoral math, these are constituent communities with specific needs around economic mobility, healthcare access, and educational opportunity that deserve direct and substantive engagement, not generic outreach or assumption of passive loyalty." },
          { name: "Younger Renter Constituency", tag: "careful", why: "The 29% renter rate in a district with a 35.0 median age signals a meaningful population of younger working adults who are economically active but not yet asset-anchored — a group whose political behavior is less predictable than the homeowner majority and more responsive to economic conditions. At $76,920 median income, the gap between earning and owning is a real and present concern for this cohort. However, engaging renters' concerns without appearing to prioritize them over the homeowner majority requires careful framing: the message must be about economic mobility and the path to stability, not renter-versus-owner positioning that alienates the 71% of residents who are already homeowners." },
          { name: "Treating 57% as a Comfortable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 57-43 result in a 77.78% white district is not a landslide — it is a held margin in a constituency where economic conditions, minority turnout, and younger voter mobilization can all move in the same direction against an incumbent simultaneously. The 43% opposition represents a substantial and demographically identifiable coalition; it did not appear by accident and will not disappear without active attention to what drove it. Any candidate who interprets 57% as a stable floor and campaigns accordingly — consolidating the existing base without addressing the concerns of the 43% — is misreading the structural vulnerability of this seat." },
          { name: "Abstract Ideological Positioning Disconnected from Economic Specifics", tag: "avoid", why: "A district with 25.3% college attainment, a $76,920 median income, and a 35.0 median age is not a constituency that responds primarily to ideological signaling — it is a constituency that evaluates on whether government is delivering concrete results for working families. Campaigns built around abstract partisan positioning rather than specific economic policy will underperform in HD-71 because the district's demographic profile — younger, working-class, with meaningful minority populations — rewards specificity and punishes detachment from material conditions. At 57-43, there is no margin available to absorb the cost of a campaign that mistakes partisan identification for constituent connection." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Louisiana HD-71: A 57-43 Republican Hold in a Majority-White, Working-Homeowner District Where the Black Constituency and Economic Anxiety Are the Margin-Defining Variables",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "HD-71 is a structurally Republican district — 57% in 2023 — but the 43% Democratic opposition in a 77.78% white, 71% homeowner-dominant constituency at $76,920 median income is not trivial noise. That income figure sits comfortably in Louisiana's middle class, but the 25.3% college attainment rate signals a working and trades-oriented workforce where economic concerns are concrete and material rather than credential-driven. The 35.0 median age is relatively young, and the 29% renter rate — while not dominant — suggests a meaningful population of younger working adults who have not yet accumulated the asset-holding stability that typically anchors Republican loyalty. The 10.7% Black population is the district's most significant minority community, and in a district where the margin is 14 points rather than 30, their turnout and candidate preference is a genuine mathematical variable for anyone seeking to either hold or flip this seat.",
          "The demographic accounting leaves a small but notable gap: White (77.78%), Black (10.7%), Hispanic (8.62%), and Asian (1.05%) sum to approximately 98.15%, suggesting a residual ~1.85% that may include American Indian or Alaska Native residents — not a dominant swing factor here, but worth noting for completeness. The more strategically significant observation is the combination of the 8.62% Hispanic population and the 10.7% Black population, which together constitute roughly 19% of the district and, if mobilized together with the younger renter cohort, represent a coalition capable of pressuring the 57% margin meaningfully. The $76,920 income and 29% renter rate together suggest a district where housing affordability, job quality, and the cost of living are active concerns rather than theoretical ones — and where the incumbent's ability to deliver on bread-and-butter economic issues, rather than ideological positioning, will determine whether 57% holds or erodes."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Treat the 43% opposition as the strategic map: in a 77.78% white district at $76,920 income, that level of opposition almost certainly reflects a coalition of Black voters, younger renters, and economically anxious working-class households — identify which of these is most activated and speak directly to their material concerns.",
          "Lead on workforce and middle-income economic security: with only 25.3% college attainment and a $76,920 median income, this is a trades- and working-professional constituency whose policy concerns center on job quality, healthcare costs, and housing affordability — not abstract economic theory.",
          "Build specific, substantive relationships with the Black community (10.7%) and Hispanic community (8.62%): these two populations together represent nearly 20% of the district, and in a 57-43 race their combined civic engagement is sufficient to determine the outcome if a credible challenge consolidates them.",
          "Do not campaign on ideology when economics will do: the 35.0 median age and 29% renter rate signal a younger constituency that is evaluating on whether government is delivering for them — a candidate who speaks concretely about housing costs, workforce development, and healthcare access will outperform one who relies on partisan identity alone."
        ]
