          { name: "Older Homeowner Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 43.3 median age and a 14% renter rate — meaning roughly 86% of households own their homes — HD-74 is a constituency whose economic security is tied to fixed assets, rising healthcare costs, and retirement income sustainability. With a $68,528 median income that is comfortable but not insulated from cost-of-living pressure, older homeowners in this district face real exposure on Medicare sustainability, prescription drug affordability, and the viability of rural and community healthcare facilities. These are the issues most capable of explaining a 46% opposition share in an otherwise demographically Republican-favorable district, and they are the issues most amenable to specific legislative remedies. A candidate who speaks to these concerns with concrete policy proposals — not ideological abstraction — is speaking directly to the material reality of HD-74's majority constituency." },
          { name: "Workforce Development and Practical Economic Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 31.6% college attainment, a clear majority of working-age adults in HD-74 built their economic lives through vocational training, skilled trades, or direct workforce entry — not four-year degrees. Economic messaging that centers practical job quality, workforce training investment, and the economic infrastructure that supports non-college-educated workers resonates directly with the district's majority experience. At $68,528 median income, the district is not economically distressed, but it is not insulated from the structural pressures facing working-class and middle-income households in Louisiana's economy. A legislative agenda that takes skills-based economic development, trade and vocational education, and rural business infrastructure seriously is both substantively responsive and electorally effective in this constituency." },
          { name: "Hispanic, Black, and AIAN Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The combined Hispanic (6.37%), Black (6.17%), and likely AIAN (~4.4% demographic gap) communities represent approximately 17% of HD-74's population — a share that, at a 54-46 margin, is arithmetically significant even if no single group is large enough to independently flip the district. Systematic neglect of these communities in constituent service, policy attention, or campaign outreach does not require active hostility to produce electoral consequences; it simply requires that a mobilized alternative demonstrate visible responsiveness to their concerns. In a district this competitive, a candidate who holds the existing 54% coalition while allowing minority community engagement to atrophy is accepting unnecessary electoral risk. Genuine constituent service — not performative outreach — is the appropriate strategic posture." },
          { name: "Treating 54% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 54-46 result in a district that is 82.79% white, approximately 86% homeowner, and above the state median income is not a strong majority — it is a margin that underperforms the district's demographic potential for a Republican candidate and signals that a significant slice of the natural coalition is already voting elsewhere. In Louisiana's legislative context, a district with these demographics should be producing margins closer to 60-65% for a well-aligned Republican incumbent; the gap between that expectation and the actual 54% is the most important diagnostic fact about HD-74. Any candidate or incumbent who interprets a 54% result in this demographic environment as a comfortable hold is misreading the competitive reality, and risks losing the seat in a cycle where economic frustration, minority community mobilization, or a high-quality opponent provides a credible alternative." },
          { name: "Abstract Ideological Messaging Disconnected from Local Economics", tag: "avoid", why: "With 31.6% college attainment and a median age of 43.3, HD-74's constituency is not primarily motivated by ideological abstraction — it is motivated by concrete economic concerns about healthcare costs, household financial security, and whether government is delivering practical results. In a district where 46% of voters already chose the opposition despite a demographically favorable environment for the incumbent, messaging that prioritizes ideological signaling over specific policy responsiveness to older homeowners' material concerns risks widening rather than closing the opposition share. The $68,528 median income places these households in a financially engaged but not comfortable position — they are watching what their representative actually does on healthcare, property rights, and local economic infrastructure, not what ideological team they signal membership in." }
