          { name: "Rural Economic Development & Workforce Investment", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $42,776 median household income and 12.7% college attainment — both indicators of a low-wage, low-credential workforce — the district's economic anxiety is the dominant political reality beneath the 70-30 partisan surface. Job creation, vocational and technical training pathways, and rural business development are the issues most directly connected to whether residents' material lives improve. A legislator who can point to specific workforce or economic development wins for HD-75 builds the kind of durable constituent loyalty that holds a seat against future challengers in ways that partisan margin alone does not." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access & Medicaid", tag: "lean-into", why: "A $42,776 median income in rural Louisiana places a substantial share of the district's households in the range where healthcare coverage is either unaffordable or Medicaid-dependent, and the 41.0 median age means a significant portion of the working-age population is dealing with chronic health conditions before Medicare eligibility. Rural hospital viability, clinic access, and the coverage gap are not ideological abstractions in a district at this income level — they are immediate family financial concerns. With 29.66% Black residents who face disproportionate rates of chronic illness and healthcare barriers, this issue has both cross-demographic resonance and specific urgency for the district's largest minority community." },
          { name: "Black Constituent Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "Nearly three in ten HD-75 residents are Black — a community that at 29.66% is large enough to be a decisive swing factor if mobilized differently, yet whose presence in the 70-30 result is ambiguous. Whether Wyble's margin reflects genuine crossover support, suppressed turnout, or both is the most important unanswered question about this district's political stability. Ignoring this constituency is a governance failure and a latent electoral risk; overclaiming a relationship with it without specific, substantive constituent service and policy attention will ring hollow. The correct approach is deliberate, consistent, issue-specific engagement on economic mobility and healthcare — not tokenism, and not neglect." },
          { name: "Treating 70% as a Signal of Policy Satisfaction", tag: "avoid", why: "A 70-30 margin in a $42,776-income district with 12.7% college attainment is better read as a reflection of partisan default and low opposition investment than as constituent endorsement of the incumbent's legislative record. Low-income, low-credential constituencies that feel economically stagnant are capable of rapid political realignment when a credible alternative appears — Louisiana's recent statewide electoral history includes multiple examples of large margins collapsing when economic frustration finds a candidate. Any holder of HD-75 who governs as though the 70% gives them room to coast is misreading the underlying constituency conditions." },
          { name: "Urban Policy Frames or Credential-Economy Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "At 12.7% college attainment — among the lowest possible levels for a state legislative district — messaging built around knowledge economy, professional workforce, or higher education credential attainment as the primary economic ladder will land as culturally alien and politically counterproductive. This is a constituency of working-age adults in trades, agriculture, service work, and hourly employment for whom college is not the path and never was; a legislator who speaks to their economic reality through that lens is signaling a mismatch between their experience and their representative's worldview. The economic message must be grounded in wages, jobs, healthcare, and rural infrastructure — not in credential attainment or professional mobility frameworks that describe someone else's life." }
