        issues: [
          { name: "Fiscal Governance and Homeowner Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $89,204 median household income and a renter rate of only 23.9%, HD-77 is a district of established homeowners with significant material stakes in property values, local tax policy, and the quality of publicly funded services. College-educated homeowners at this income level evaluate their representative on whether government is well-managed and whether their community is improving — not on abstract ideology. A legislative record anchored to transparent fiscal stewardship, infrastructure investment, and local government efficiency speaks directly to the dominant constituency's self-interest and is difficult for a challenger to attack." },
          { name: "Education and Workforce Quality", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 43.7% college attainment rate is a strong signal that education quality — both K-12 and higher education — is a personal and economic priority for a substantial portion of the district's adults. At a 39.2 median age, many residents have school-age children or recent graduates entering the workforce, making the quality-to-cost equation of public education a live household concern. A legislator who can speak specifically to education funding, teacher retention, and the local workforce pipeline earns credibility with the constituency's most economically engaged and politically informed segment. This is also an issue where inaction or perceived neglect creates the clearest opening for a future challenger." },
          { name: "Black and Hispanic Community Constituent Service", tag: "careful", why: "The district's 7.89% Black and 5.82% Hispanic populations — nearly 14% combined — have never had their electoral potential tested in HD-77, given the unopposed 2023 result. In a district where the margin has never been stress-tested, these communities represent the most plausible foundation for a future competitive challenge, particularly if a candidate emerges who makes specific constituent service to them a centerpiece. Proactive, substantive engagement — not token outreach — is the strategic hedge against that scenario; it also reflects a genuine governance obligation to residents whose concerns may be underrepresented in an uncontested political environment. The careful framing is warranted because the approach must be authentic and issue-specific rather than performative, or it will not move voters." },
          { name: "Assuming the Unopposed Result Reflects Deep Satisfaction", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed win at 100% generates no information about actual support depth, issue salience, or where dissatisfaction is accumulating — it means no one ran, not that everyone approves. A 43.7% college-attainment constituency at $89,204 income is fully capable of recruiting and funding a credible challenger if a sufficient number of residents conclude the incumbent is coasting or unresponsive. Reading the 2023 result as a stable floor rather than a competitive vacuum leads directly to the kind of low-engagement incumbent behavior that creates the opening a challenger needs." },
          { name: "Neglecting Minority Community Concerns as Electorally Irrelevant", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district that has never been contested, the 14% combined Black and Hispanic population has functioned as politically invisible — but that invisibility is a product of the electoral context, not a permanent condition. If a future challenger makes specific outreach to these communities a strategic priority, the incumbent who has never built those relationships will be starting from zero against someone who has already made the investment. Beyond electoral risk, governing a district with nearly 14% minority population as though those constituents' concerns don't require specific attention is both a representational failure and the kind of neglect that eventually generates organized opposition." }
        ],
