          { name: "Homeowner Economic Security & Cost of Living", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $75,069 median household income and an estimated 70% homeownership rate, HD-78's dominant constituency is middle-income property owners for whom rising insurance premiums, infrastructure reliability, and municipal service costs are immediate financial concerns — not abstractions. Louisiana's property insurance crisis has hit homeowners at precisely this income level hardest, as they lack the financial cushion of higher earners but bear the full exposure of ownership. A legislative record that produces specific, visible results on insurance reform, road and drainage infrastructure, and local tax fairness is the strongest possible incumbent protection in a district that has not yet been tested by a competitive race. Concrete deliverables on homeowner costs are the most durable foundation for a seat that has so far won by default." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Constituent Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 19.3%, the Hispanic community is HD-78's largest non-white demographic and represents a constituency whose long-term electoral trajectory is upward as participation rates continue to grow. An uncontested 2023 race means this community's political preferences have not been tested against an alternative — they were never asked to choose. A representative who builds authentic constituent relationships with Hispanic residents now, through specific attention to workforce, healthcare access, and community infrastructure concerns, converts a passive non-opposition into an active coalition before competitive pressure ever arrives. Ignoring this constituency in a district where no opponent currently exists is borrowing against future electoral risk at a high rate." },
          { name: "Renter Population & Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "The 30.3% renter rate represents roughly three in ten HD-78 households — a constituency with materially different economic concerns than the homeowner majority, and one that skews younger given the 41.7 median age and typical renter demographics. In an uncontested race, renters' preferences were never forced to the surface, but they represent the most organizationally accessible base for a future challenger running on housing affordability, economic precarity, or cost-of-living frustration. Engaging renter concerns — without framing it as a conflict with homeowner interests — allows the incumbent to consolidate the full district rather than conceding a third of it as implicitly opposed. The careful lane here is that renter-focused policy can be framed as community economic health rather than redistributive tension with property owners." },
          { name: "Campaigning on Partisan Identity Alone", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% result from an uncontested race provides zero information about how partisan identity messaging performs with this specific constituency — it is analytically empty as a strategic signal. HD-78's $75,069 income, 30.9% college attainment, and 30.3% renter rate describe a district with genuine economic complexity that spans demographic communities, and residents in this income bracket tend to evaluate on delivered results rather than ideological alignment when given a real choice. A campaign or governance posture built primarily on partisan identity rather than specific local deliverables will be structurally vulnerable the first time a credible opponent appears with a concrete local agenda. The uncontested result should produce urgency to build substantive depth, not complacency built on an unrevealing margin." },
          { name: "Treating the Seat as Safe Because It Was Uncontested", tag: "avoid", why: "Uncontested results are frequently misread as expressions of constituent satisfaction when they are more often expressions of organizational failure on the opposition side — the absence of a challenge in 2023 reflects the Democratic Party's resource allocation and recruitment capacity in that cycle, not a judgment on the incumbent's governing record. HD-78's demographic profile — a quarter of residents from non-white communities, 30% renters, and a Hispanic population approaching 20% — contains the raw material for a competitive challenge if an opponent with resources and a targeted local message emerges. A representative who governs as though 100% is a constituency mandate rather than a structural artifact will have built none of the cross-community relationships needed to survive a real race, and will find the district's untested competitive dynamics fully exposed at the worst possible moment." }
