          { name: "Housing Costs and Renter Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 33.2% renter rate in a district with $77,984 median household income is an anomaly that signals genuine cost pressure in the rental market — these are not primarily low-income renters but working and professional households priced out of homeownership or choosing flexibility in a competitive housing environment. One-third of constituents have a direct material stake in rental affordability, housing supply, and tenant protections that the homeowner majority does not share. This is the most concrete economic cleavage in the district's data, and any challenger who frames it effectively has a ready-made organizing issue. The incumbent must engage it proactively rather than allowing it to become opposition territory." },
          { name: "College-Educated Constituent Accountability", tag: "careful", why: "At 43.8% college attainment, HD-79's electorate is more educated than most Louisiana state house districts, and educated voters — particularly at the upper-middle income level of $77,984 — tend to evaluate incumbents on legislative record, policy competence, and constituent responsiveness rather than partisan loyalty alone. This is a double-edged asset: the incumbent benefits from this constituency's preference for stability and competence, but the same voters will respond negatively to perceived incompetence, scandal, or inattention. An unopposed 2023 race means the incumbent has not yet had to defend a legislative record before a skeptical, informed audience — that test may come, and the preparation for it must begin well before a challenger files." },
          { name: "Assuming Unopposed Means Unassailable", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2023 unopposed result creates a false sense of electoral security that is one of the most dangerous conditions in state legislative politics. No tested floor, no coalition data, no revealed vulnerabilities — the absence of opposition is not evidence of strength, it is evidence of an untested hypothesis. The demographic inputs for a competitive challenge — a 20% Hispanic community, a 33% renter population, a 9.55% Black community, and an educated electorate that evaluates on competence — are all present and waiting for a credible candidate to organize them. Any incumbent who campaigns on the assumption that 2023's result will repeat in a contested cycle is making a bet the data does not support." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Black Community at 9.55%", tag: "avoid", why: "At 9.55% of the district, the Black community is not numerically dominant, but in a contested race where the incumbent has no proven margin, every constituency bloc matters at the margin. The demographic sum of white (57.09%), Hispanic (20.06%), Black (9.55%), and Asian (6.54%) leaves a small residual gap suggesting some American Indian or Alaska Native presence as well, meaning no single community can be safely deprioritized. Constituent service failures, legislative inattention, or visible disengagement from Black community concerns in a district this demographically diverse will not stay quiet — they will become recruiting tools for the first credible challenger who shows up with a different offer." }
