          { name: "Renter Protections & Housing Stability", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 38.3% of residents renting in a district where median household income is $40,463, housing cost instability is one of the most direct threats to constituent financial security — a rent increase, eviction, or loss of housing subsidy is a crisis at this income level in a way it is not at twice the income. Legislative attention to affordable housing supply, eviction prevention, and tenant rights is not abstract policy for this constituency; it is protection for more than a third of the district's households. In a majority-Black, low-income district, housing stability is also a racial equity issue with deep historical resonance, and a representative who engages it with that specificity earns a different kind of constituent loyalty than one who treats it as a budget line." },
          { name: "Constituent Complacency Risk", tag: "careful", why: "An unopposed race is the strongest possible electoral result — and one of the most strategically dangerous postures for long-term representation. When there is no opponent to organize against, constituent mobilization atrophies, and the representative's accountability relationship shifts from electoral to institutional. In a district with $40,463 median income, 12.2% college attainment, and 38.3% renters, unmet material needs accumulate quietly until they produce either a credible challenger or, more commonly, a disengaged electorate that stops showing up. The careful move is to treat the unopposed win as a mandate for delivery, not as a signal that the district is permanently secured." },
          { name: "Treating the Unopposed Win as a Measure of Strength", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed result measures the absence of opposition, not the depth of constituent satisfaction. In HD-96, with one of the lowest college attainment rates in any district and a median income well below what is needed for housing and healthcare security, the risk is not an opposing candidate in 2025 — it is a slow erosion of trust among constituents who see their material conditions unchanged. Any messaging or governing posture that implicitly relies on 'I ran unopposed' as evidence of performance is both analytically wrong and politically corrosive in a constituency where delivering results is the only real accountability mechanism." },
          { name: "Abstract or Ideological Legislative Positioning", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 12.2% of adults hold college degrees and median household income is $40,463, abstract ideological positioning — culture-war framings, procedural legislative victories, national partisan signaling — lands with the least resonance of any messaging strategy. This constituency evaluates representation concretely: did the school get funding, did the jobs come, did the housing get built, did the healthcare stay open. A legislative record built on procedural wins or ideological positioning without tangible district-level outcomes will not survive the scrutiny of a constituency that lives close enough to the economic margin that abstractions feel like indifference." }
