          { name: "Housing Security and Renter Protections", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 39.1% renter rate in a district with a $56,103 median household income and a majority-Black constituency, housing insecurity is not a background condition — it is the primary economic vulnerability for a substantial share of residents. Black and Hispanic renters in Louisiana face disproportionate exposure to eviction, rent increases, and displacement without the asset buffer of homeownership that protects wealthier households. Legislative action on tenant rights, eviction diversion programs, affordable housing production, and code enforcement accountability directly addresses the material reality of roughly four in ten HD-101 households. This is the issue where Lafleur can build the most visible, durable constituent relationship between legislative action and neighborhood-level outcome." },
          { name: "Workforce Development and Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 71.1% of adults in HD-101 lacking a four-year college degree and a median age of 32.5, the district's workforce is young, working-age, and navigating an economy that increasingly requires credentialed skills that four-year degrees are not the only pathway to acquire. Community college access, vocational and technical training pipelines, apprenticeship programs, and employer partnership initiatives are the economic mobility tools most directly relevant to a constituency at this income and education intersection. At $56,103 median household income — solid but not secure in Louisiana's cost environment — the difference between a credentialed and non-credentialed worker is often the difference between financial stability and chronic precarity. This is a constituency that rewards a legislator who shows up with specific programs, named employers, and documented outcomes — not abstract economic development rhetoric." },
          { name: "Black Community Institutional Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "At 59.84% Black, HD-101's constituency is not a demographic to be acknowledged — it is the governing majority whose churches, HBCUs, civic organizations, and community institutions are the infrastructure through which political trust is built and sustained. The risk of an unopposed incumbent is institutional drift: relationships with community anchors that were strong during the campaign cycle weaken during the governing cycle as the representative's attention moves to the legislative arena. Maintaining active, specific engagement with Black institutional leadership — not performative visits but genuine policy co-development — is the mechanism that converts electoral consensus into governing legitimacy. If Lafleur's record on issues like criminal justice, healthcare access, and economic development is not visible and legible to Black institutional leaders in the district, the consensus that produced the 100% result becomes fragile." },
          { name: "Treating Uncontested Margin as Governing Permission to Disengage", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% uncontested result in 2023 contains no information about constituent satisfaction — only about the absence of organized opposition at that moment. In majority-Black, majority-renter districts with young populations and economic stress, the conditions for a credible primary challenger are always present: a community organizer, a local pastor, a union leader, or a younger candidate who can credibly argue the incumbent is not fighting hard enough. Louisiana's legislative primary system means the real electoral threat is never the general election — it is the primary, and a primary challenger in HD-101 would not need to peel off white conservatives but simply to activate a portion of the district's young, economically stressed majority who feel underserved. Governing as though 100% is a stable floor is the fastest path to making it untrue." },
          { name: "Abstract Legislative Positioning Disconnected from District Economics", tag: "avoid", why: "A district with a $56,103 median income, 39.1% renter rate, 28.9% college attainment, and a 32.5-year median age is a constituency that evaluates its representative on tangible, proximate economic outcomes — not ideological positioning, statewide political profile, or legislative leadership titles. At this income level in Louisiana, the gap between what government delivers and what households need is concrete and daily: a housing repair that didn't happen, a workforce program that wasn't available, a healthcare bill that wasn't covered. Any legislative agenda that prioritizes abstract policy wins visible only in Baton Rouge over specific, named, district-level deliverables will produce exactly the kind of quiet disillusionment that, in an uncontested district, shows up not as a lost election but as declining turnout and a community that has stopped believing its representative is fighting for them." }
