          "HD-104 is a firmly Republican district — Gallé won with 64% in 2023 — whose demographic architecture largely explains the margin. At 71.64% white, 80.8% homeownership (the inverse of the 19.2% renter rate), $78,561 median household income, and 38.7 median age, this is a middle-income, middle-aged, homeowner-dominant constituency whose material interests and political habits are well-aligned with the incumbent's coalition. The 30.7% college attainment rate is modest but consistent with a working-professional and trades-skilled homeowner base rather than a university-town profile. The remaining demographic categories — 14.16% Black, 9.16% Hispanic/Latino, 0.94% Asian — account for approximately 24.26% of the reported population, and the gap to 100% suggests a small but nonzero American Indian or Alaska Native presence that should be noted even if it is not electorally decisive at this margin. The 36% Democratic share is real but structurally contained: it does not threaten the seat in a single cycle, but it represents a constituency that exists and is being served — or not — by the current representative.",
          "The strategic reality of HD-104 is that the 64-36 margin creates a false sense of immunity. At $78,561 median income with 80.8% homeownership, the district's majority constituency is financially stable but not insulated — rising insurance costs, property taxes, infrastructure quality, and healthcare access are the pressure points for a middle-income homeowner community that expects government to protect what they have built. The 14.16% Black constituency is the district's most structurally distinct minority bloc: large enough to be a governing obligation, concentrated enough to be a mobilization target if neglected, and historically underserved enough that authentic engagement has genuine electoral upside. At 9.16% Hispanic/Latino, that community is smaller but present and growing in most comparable Louisiana districts, meaning its long-term trajectory matters for durable coalition-building. A candidate who holds this seat by consolidating only the white homeowner majority while ignoring the 14% Black constituency is holding it on borrowed time — not because the math flips immediately, but because a credible Democratic challenger who runs up the score in that community while peeling off economically frustrated white homeowners can close the gap faster than a 64-36 headline suggests."
