          "The economic and tenure profile of HD-105 adds critical texture. At $79,231 median household income — relatively comfortable by Louisiana standards — with a 30.6% renter rate, 27.9% college attainment, and 37.1 median age, this is a working-to-middle-class district of moderate educational attainment with a meaningful renter minority alongside a homeowner majority. The income level suggests a constituency that is economically functional but not insulated from cost-of-living pressure: housing costs, insurance rates, and healthcare access are live concerns rather than abstract ones. Louisiana's notoriously high insurance premiums — both auto and homeowners — hit a $79,231-income household with real force, and the 30.6% renter share represents constituents who face housing affordability pressure more acutely than owners. The 37.1 median age points to a constituency in early-to-mid family-formation stage, for whom school quality, childcare costs, and neighborhood safety are not just policy abstractions but daily household decisions. A candidate who speaks to these material conditions with specificity, across racial lines, is building the cross-demographic coalition that the 56% result suggests is at least partially achievable."
