          { name: "Abstract or Ideological Messaging Disconnected from Economic Conditions", tag: "avoid", why: "A 43% college-educated, majority-renter, working-age district at $56,840 median income is not a constituency that responds to symbolic positioning or ideological abstraction — it is a constituency that evaluates on housing costs, job quality, healthcare access, and whether public institutions are functioning. With no electoral competition forcing message discipline, there is a structural risk of drifting toward the kind of performative legislative presence that substitutes visibility for delivery. The specific combination of educational attainment and income constraint in this district means residents have both the analytical capacity to assess legislative output and the personal economic stakes to demand it." }
