          "The 43% college-educated adult share is notably high for a district at $56,840 median income — a gap that signals either a young, credentialed workforce that has not yet reached peak earning years, or a constituency where educational attainment has not translated into commensurate economic returns, or both. The 38.1 median age and 55.4% renter rate together reinforce the picture of a working-age constituency navigating housing cost pressure in one of the South's most dynamic and expensive urban rental markets. A district that is 50% Black, majority renter, and college-educated above its income level is a constituency with rising expectations, civic infrastructure, and the organizational capacity to evaluate performance — which means that running unopposed is not a mandate for inaction but rather a baseline of trust that must be earned and renewed through demonstrable legislative output on the issues that shape daily life for working renters."
