        issues: [
          { name: "Renter Protections and Housing Cost Stability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 49.3% renter rate and $33,896 median household income, nearly half of SD-34's residents are renters with limited financial buffer against rent increases, eviction, or housing instability. This is not a peripheral constituency concern — it describes the daily economic reality of the district's plurality. Rental cost increases, habitability standards, eviction prevention, and access to housing assistance programs are direct material issues for a constituency at this income level. A legislator who can point to specific actions on tenant protections or housing cost relief has a durable claim on the trust that produced a 71% margin." },
          { name: "Economic Mobility and Workforce Quality for Working-Age Adults", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 35.6 median age places SD-34 squarely in a working-age constituency whose primary economic concerns are wages, job quality, and upward mobility — not retirement security or early-career student debt. At 15.8% college attainment and $33,896 median income, the district's workforce is largely non-credentialed and concentrated in sectors with high cost-of-living exposure. Workforce development, vocational training access, wage floor policy, and employer accountability are the economic mobility levers most directly relevant to this constituency's actual life circumstances. These are the issues that sustain the 71% coalition because they address the underlying conditions that make voters trust or distrust their representative." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access for Low-Income Residents", tag: "careful", why: "At $33,896 median income and 15.8% college attainment, SD-34's constituency is highly exposed to gaps in healthcare coverage, Medicaid access, and the cost of uninsured care. This is an issue where the potential political reward is high — but where the gap between legislative aspiration and actual delivery is visible and measurable to constituents who are directly experiencing the consequences. Jackson must be specific about what actions have produced or will produce concrete improvements in coverage and access, not rhetorical about healthcare as a value. Vague commitment without tangible results will be evaluated skeptically by a constituency accustomed to campaign promises outrunning delivery." },
          { name: "Abstract or Ideological Legislative Positioning", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where median household income is $33,896 and nearly half of residents are renters, constituents are not evaluating their state senator on ideological consistency or partisan signaling — they are evaluating on material outcomes. A legislative profile dominated by symbolic votes, culture-war positioning, or abstract policy debates that produce no tangible benefit for low-income working families will erode the economic trust that is the actual foundation of the 71% result. The district's demographics do not produce automatic loyalty — they produce loyalty to a representative who visibly delivers. Losing that specificity is the primary route to a weakened coalition." },
          { name: "Treating 71% as a Permanent Ceiling Rather Than a Coalition to Be Maintained", tag: "avoid", why: "Jackson's 71% in 2023 is strong, but in a district with 49.3% renters and a $33,896 median income, voter mobility — both geographic and political — is structurally higher than in asset-wealthy homeowner districts. Renter constituencies move more frequently, require more continuous voter file maintenance, and are more likely to disengage when they perceive their representative as inaccessible or ineffective. A campaign posture that treats 71% as a durable floor rather than a coalition that must be actively renewed each cycle is the strategic error most likely to produce a competitive race from a direction that current data does not suggest is coming — but could arrive quickly if economic trust is not sustained." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Louisiana SD-34: A 66% Black, Low-Income, Renter-Heavy District Where Jackson's 71% Coalition Was Built on Turnout and Economic Trust — and Must Be Maintained the Same Way",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "LA-SD-34 is one of the most demographically concentrated districts in this analysis: 66.2% Black, 29.09% white, with a $33,896 median household income that places it firmly in Louisiana's lower-income tier, a 15.8% college attainment rate, and a 49.3% renter rate that signals a constituency with limited asset wealth and high exposure to housing cost instability. Jackson's 71-29 margin in 2023 is not a soft plurality — it is a commanding coalition result, but in a district with this income profile and this renter concentration, that coalition is built on perceived economic relevance, not on abstract partisan loyalty. Voters at $33,896 median income who are nearly half renters are asking a very direct question of their representative: are you actually doing something about the conditions of our daily lives? The 71% result is the reward for answering yes convincingly; the risk is that it can erode if that answer becomes less credible.",
          "The 29% white minority in this district — nearly a third of the population — deserves analytical attention beyond its share of the partisan result. In a district where the Black community at 66.2% is the clear political majority, the white constituency's 29% share is large enough to be a meaningful factor in close cycles and in coalition-building for broader legislative influence. The 35.6 median age signals a working-age constituency — not a retirement-income district and not a youth-dominated one — which means the dominant economic concerns are wages, employment quality, healthcare access, and the cost of housing rather than retirement security or student debt specifically. The 15.8% college attainment rate, the lowest-quartile income, and the near-50% renter rate together describe a community where economic precarity is structural and where a legislator's credibility rests on concrete material outcomes, not ideological signaling."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Anchor every public message to the $33,896 median income and 49.3% renter rate — this constituency evaluates its representative on material economic outcomes: rental assistance, wage protections, healthcare access, and utility costs are not peripheral issues, they are the core test of whether Jackson deserves the coalition that produced 71%.",
          "Invest in turnout infrastructure for the 66.2% Black constituency specifically — the 71% margin was earned, not guaranteed, and in a low-income district with high renter mobility, the voter file requires continuous maintenance and community-level engagement to hold that coalition together across election cycles.",
          "Engage the 29% white minority with consistent constituent service rather than treating it as an opposition bloc — in a district this economically homogeneous across race lines, shared working-class economic concerns around housing costs, healthcare, and employment quality are more politically unifying than the partisan margin suggests.",
          "Avoid any governing posture that looks disconnected from low-income renter concerns — with nearly half the district renting at incomes well below state median, any perception that Jackson is more responsive to developer, landlord, or institutional interests than to tenants and working families is the single fastest way to erode a coalition built on economic trust."
        ]
