          { name: "Renter Housing Stability", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 32.7% renter rate at a $60,420 median income identifies a large bloc of residents who are both economically exposed to housing cost increases and structurally less likely to be anchored to the district long-term by homeownership. Renter households at this income level are one bad month or one rent increase away from displacement, and in Louisiana's current housing market, that is not a hypothetical. A candidate who makes renter protections, affordable housing investment, and utility cost stabilization into concrete legislative priorities is speaking to roughly one-in-three households with a message that has direct material relevance — and that bloc, while less reliably partisan than homeowners, delivers decisive margins when mobilized." },
          { name: "Black Voter Turnout and Community Investment", tag: "careful", why: "The 25.87% Black population is the structural foundation of the 62% Democratic margin, but its electoral leverage depends entirely on sustained turnout and genuine community investment — not assumed loyalty. In Louisiana's off-cycle and lower-salience elections, Black voter turnout is highly responsive to whether candidates have maintained authentic relationships between campaigns, delivered visible constituent services, and demonstrated accountability to community-specific concerns around economic opportunity, criminal justice, and public health. A candidate who takes this constituency for granted — assuming the 62% margin is structural rather than earned — is making the most consequential strategic error available in this district. Engagement must be year-round and specific, not mobilization-season performative." },
          { name: "Ideological or Cultural Framing That Alienates White Working-Class Voters", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district that is 63.27% white, earning 24.6% college-educated, at $60,420 income, the 38% Republican share reflects a white working-class segment that is reachable on economic grounds but will consolidate against a candidate perceived as culturally condescending or ideologically abstract. Framing that prioritizes national progressive identity politics over local economic concerns — housing costs, wages, healthcare bills — gives the Republican opposition a wedge that functions regardless of the specific candidate. The winning message in SD-36 is material and local, not ideological and national, and any deviation from that frame is a gift to the opposition." },
          { name: "Treating 62% as a Structural Ceiling Rather Than a Managed Outcome", tag: "avoid", why: "A 62% result in a district with this demographic and economic profile is not guaranteed to reproduce itself — it is the product of a specific candidate, a specific moment, and a specific level of Black voter turnout and white working-class crossover that can all move in the wrong direction simultaneously. With 32.7% renters at working-class income levels and a white majority that has demonstrated 38% Republican propensity, the variables that could compress this margin to 54-55% are all present and active. Any campaign strategy that assumes 62% is the floor, rather than a result requiring active maintenance, is analytically wrong and strategically dangerous in a district where a modest turnout or persuasion shift across either the Black or white working-class constituency changes the competitive picture entirely." }
