        memoHeadline: "Louisiana SD-36: A 62% Democratic Hold Built on a Quarter-Black Constituency, Working-Class Renters, and Below-Median Income — Durable but Not Invincible",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "LA-SD-36 is a Democratic-leaning district whose 62% Bass victory in 2023 rests on a coalition that is structurally coherent but economically stressed. The 25.87% Black population is the single most important organizing fact about this district — at roughly one-in-four residents, African American voters almost certainly represent a larger share of the active Democratic base than their raw population share suggests, given historical turnout patterns in Louisiana majority-minority coalition districts. The 63.27% white majority is numerically dominant but almost certainly internally divided: at $60,420 median household income, this is not an affluent white suburban district but a working-class and lower-middle-class white constituency that evaluates candidates on material economic concerns — wages, healthcare costs, utility bills, and local service delivery — rather than ideological abstraction. The 38% Republican share is real opposition rooted in that white working-class segment, and it is the floor that becomes a ceiling only if the Democratic candidate loses ground among Black voters or fails to peel away economically anxious white voters.",
          "The district's secondary demographic signals reinforce the working-class economic frame. A 32.7% renter rate — meaningfully above the homeowner-dominant baseline of rural Louisiana — combined with a $60,420 median income and only 24.6% college attainment identifies a constituency that is housing-cost-sensitive, wage-dependent, and operating without significant financial cushion. The 37.5 median age is neither young enough to signal a generational-change electorate nor old enough to anchor the district in fixed-income retirement concerns — it is a working-age district where the primary anxiety is economic stability, not cultural identity. The four reported demographic categories sum to approximately 96.14%, leaving a small residual that likely includes American Indian or Alaska Native residents; while not a dominant swing factor at this magnitude, it warrants acknowledgment in constituent outreach. The strategic picture is of a winnable but not guaranteed Democratic seat: 62% is a comfortable margin that could compress to 55% if turnout among Black voters softens or if the Republican candidate credibly targets white working-class economic anxiety with a specific, localized message."
        ],
