          { name: "Working-Class Housing and Renter Stability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 29.6% renter rate in a district with a $63,095 median income identifies a substantial constituency for whom housing cost is a monthly economic crisis, not an abstract policy debate. Renter households in this income range are disproportionately exposed to rising rents, eviction risk, and the absence of the wealth-building that homeownership provides — and in a district with a 34.46% Black population, these housing pressures fall hardest along racial lines. A candidate who engages housing affordability, tenant protections, and housing supply with legislative specificity is addressing both the economic anxiety of white working-class renters and the structural economic conditions facing much of the Black community simultaneously. This is a rare issue that can simultaneously consolidate the existing coalition and reach the 44% opposition. At a 56-44 margin, that dual reach is a strategic premium." },
          { name: "Cross-Racial Coalition Maintenance", tag: "careful", why: "The 56% result in a district that is 34.46% Black and 55.96% white reflects a cross-racial coalition that is not automatic and not permanent — it was built under specific conditions and must be actively maintained. Messaging or legislative positioning that tilts too heavily toward one community's concerns at the visible expense of the other creates exactly the fault line a challenger needs. At the same time, false equivalence — treating both communities identically when their economic circumstances differ — fails both. The strategic challenge is sustaining a coalition by being specific and credible with each constituency's distinct material concerns while communicating a unifying economic security frame. Any candidate who handles this clumsily, either by taking Black voters for granted or by racializing the economic message in ways that alienate white working-class voters, hands the 44% opposition a path to 50%." },
          { name: "Racially Divisive Framing on Economic Issues", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where a 56-44 result depends on cross-racial coalition alignment, any messaging that frames economic policy through explicitly racial grievance — in either direction — destabilizes the coalition that produced the current margin. White working-class households at $63,095 income and Black households facing disproportionate renter and healthcare exposure have overlapping economic interests that a unifying candidate can speak to simultaneously. A candidate who instead activates racial competition over economic resources loses the political ground that makes this seat winnable. The demographic composition of SD-38 means that racially divisive framing does not consolidate a reliable majority — it fragments one." },
          { name: "Treating 56% as a Comfortable or Structurally Safe Margin", tag: "avoid", why: "A 56-44 result in a district with 34.46% Black population is not a safe seat — it is a competitive seat whose current margin reflects a specific candidate-coalition alignment that requires active maintenance. A credible challenger who runs explicit, sustained outreach to Black voters alongside economic anxiety messaging to working-class white households has a direct mathematical path to flipping this district with a modest shift in either community's behavior. The 30% college attainment rate and $63,095 income signal a constituency that evaluates on tangible results, not partisan loyalty — and economic dissatisfaction can move these voters faster than in higher-income, higher-education districts. Any candidate who campaigns SD-38 as though 56% represents a stable floor rather than a constructed coalition outcome is arithmetically and strategically wrong." }
